{{also|Refrain}}
==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en-us-refrain.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{rhymes|eɪn}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{etyl|ang}} {{term|refreinen|lang=ang}}, {{etyl|fro}} {{term|refrener|lang=fro}}, {{etyl|fr}} {{term|refrener|lang=fr}}, from {{etyl|la}} {{term|refrenare|lang=la}}; influenced by {{etyl|fro}} {{term|refraindre||to [[restrain]], [[moderate]]|lang=fro}}, from {{etyl|LL.}} {{term|refrangere|lang=la}}, for {{etyl|la}} {{term|refringere||to break up, [[break]]|lang=la}} (see {{term|refract}}). {{etyl|la}} {{term|refrenare|lang=la}} is from prefix {{term|re-||back|lang=la}} + {{term|frenum||bridle|lang=la}}.

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{transitive}} To [[hold]] back; to [[restrain]]; to keep within prescribed [[bounds]]; to [[curb]]; to [[govern]].
#* [[w:Geoffrey_Chaucer|Chaucer]]
#*: His reson '''refraineth''' not his foul delight or talent.
#* ''Proverbs i:15''.
#*: '''Refrain''' thy foot from their path.
# {{transitive}} To abstain from
#* [[w:Thomas Browne|Thomas Browne]]
#*: Who, requiring a remedy for his gout, received no other counsel than to '''refrain''' cold drink.
# {{intransitive}} To keep one’s self from [[action]] or [[interference]]; to [[hold]] [[aloof]]; to [[forbear]]; to [[abstain]].
#* ''Acts 5:38''
#*: '''Refrain''' from these men, and let them alone.
#* [[w:Thomas Browne|Thomas Browne]]
#*: They '''refrained''' therefrom [eating flesh] some time after.

